---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE, SOCIALS } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description={`${SITE.author}: ${SITE.role}.`}>
  <div class="prose-page">
    <header class="page-header about-header">
      {SITE.avatar && (
        <div class="about-photo">
          <img src={SITE.avatar} alt={`Portrait of ${SITE.author}`} width="640" height="640" />
        </div>
      )}
      <div>
        <h1>{SITE.author}</h1>
        <p class="lede">{SITE.role}.</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I build agentic systems for a security operations team. The work sits
        where a model's output turns into a decision someone acts on: triaging
        alerts, supporting incident response, and analysing reported phishing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Security is an unforgiving place to put a language model. Inputs are
        adversarial by design, a confident wrong answer costs analyst time or
        worse, and "it looked good in the demo" is not a standard anyone will
        accept. Most of what I have learned is about the engineering around the
        model: evaluations, deterministic steps where they belong, and knowing
        when an agent is the wrong tool.
      </p>

      <h2>What I write about</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Evaluation.</strong> How to tell whether a system is getting better, from code-graded checks to grading production traffic. The <a href="/series/evals-in-practice/">Evals in Practice</a> series is the long version.</li>
        <li><strong>Agents and workflows.</strong> When to let the model choose the next step, and when that choice belongs in code.</li>
        <li><strong>Practical tooling.</strong> The small, specific pieces that make AI useful to analysts day to day.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Posts start as notes on something that went differently in production
        than it did on paper. Code samples are illustrations, trimmed to the
        idea, not drop-in software.
      </p>

      <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
      <p>
        Code is on <a href={SOCIALS.github} rel="me noopener">GitHub</a>, including
        <a class="nowrap" href="https://github.com/ftrout/secops-claude-skills">secops-claude-skills</a>,
        a set of Claude skills for security work. New posts arrive by
        <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>. If something here is wrong, I would rather
        know: open an issue on the
        <a href="https://github.com/ftrout/ftrout.github.io/issues">site's repository</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
